<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kabarda</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1825 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The most powerful Circassian principality, on the plains of the central North Caucasus, an early ally of Muscovy and a major regional power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kabarda formed in the 15th century when Circassian princes, traditionally descended from the semi-legendary Inal, settled the plains along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terek-river">Terek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malka-river">Malka</a> and Baksan rivers. Its princes extended their power over neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ossetians">Ossetians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingush-people">Ingush</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkars">Balkars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abazins">Abazins</a>. Russian and Ottoman sources speak of Kabarda as a distinct country from the 16th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kabarda was ruled by an aristocracy of princes and nobles who held peasants and serfs, with assemblies settling major questions. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temryuk-idarov">Temryuk Idarov</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a>, who married his daughter Maria in 1561. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabardians">Kabardians</a> bred famous horses and set standards of etiquette admired across the region. Their forces defeated a Crimean army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kanzhal">Kanzhal</a> in 1708.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Divided into Greater and Lesser Kabarda, it became a battleground between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a>. Russian forts from 1763 and the campaigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksey-yermolov">General Yermolov</a> after 1818 broke its independence, and by 1825 it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1450 CE – 1825 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:7.46%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1825 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Temryuk Idarov, Kurgoqo Atajuq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kabardian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kabarda, Kabardia, Greater and Lesser Kabarda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1638&amp;focus=principality-of-kabarda" aria-label="Principality of Kabarda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5630 235 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kabarda lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5955 888h0M5820 533h0M5808 852h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5950 491h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5950 491h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1638&amp;focus=principality-of-kabarda">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

